AB = \sqrt{BC^2 - AC^2} = \sqrt{7^2 - 4^2} = \sqrt{49 - 16} = \sqrt{33}
\] Since \(\angle A = 90^\circ\), we know that \(\angle B + \angle C = 90^\circ\). Therefore, \(\angle C = 90^\circ - \angle B\). In terms of cosine, we know that \(\cos(90^\circ - \angle B) = \sin(\angle B)\). So, \(\cos C = \sin B = \frac{4}{7}\). </think> <answer> \frac{4}{7} </answer>

